    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d321">

                <pb facs="0490" n="410" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg410"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.114 Belgium/224a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Belgium</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Morris</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-10T18:00:00-05:00">March 10, 1936—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>10. Department’s instruction No. 320, February 4, 1936.<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d321-f1"/> Belgian Ambassador appears to be favorably impressed by
                    our arguments for cooperation to suppress alcohol smuggling and stated that he
                    is planning an approach to his Government which he hopes will be successful in
                    obtaining action we desire. He asks that in the meantime we make no further
                    direct efforts ourselves.</p>
                <pb facs="0491" n="411" xml:id="pg411"/>
                <p>Please disregard, therefore, the request in our instruction No. 332 of March 10
                    to do whatever you can to facilitate the Ambassador’s efforts, and omit any
                    further reference to this subject for the present in your relations with the
                    Belgian Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
